Eighteenth system tracing expansion (2026-10-13)
==============================================

13 new kernel contracts in four source files, at 19 source sites (18 in the
native kernel; the additional socket helper-hook site requires SOCKET_HHOOK).
Four profiles bring the bsdinstruments base installation catalog to 438.
No installation occurred. Inventory: coverage-eighteenth.json.
All new selectors are sdt:::NAME. Counts are unique provider/module/function/
name contracts, not runtime instances, source sites or generated FBT probes.

Scope and placement
-------------------
Socket allocation failures, socket buffer limits, blocking accept, filesystem
write throttling, AIO process rundown, list waits and completion waits.
Existing generic operation/result probes do not isolate these causal failures
or individual waits. New events only occur on failures or actual sleeps, not
successful packet or storage I/O fast paths. No new allocator-internal probe,
payload copying, allocation, counter update or lock acquisition is introduced.

Every event excludes cold startup. Allocation and socket-buffer failures also
exclude stopped-scheduler and raw interrupt contexts. The sleep events surround
existing sleepable thread operations; no probe was placed in an interrupt
filter, panic handler or dump completion path. Some events fire under existing
locks and may extend their hold time when enabled. Live overhead is unmeasured.

Contracts (arguments in order)
-----------------------------
sockbuf-reserve-failure: socket identity, SO_RCV/SO_SND selector, cause,
requested bytes, applicable byte limit.
  cause 1 = request exceeds BUF_MAX_ADJ(buf_max), the effective buffer cap.
  cause 2 = chgsbsize rejects per-user buffer accounting under RLIMIT_SBSIZE.
The latter limit is the caller's supplied thread limit; when no appropriate
thread exists, the existing code supplies RLIM_INFINITY. No credential or UID
is exposed. The shared reservation path covers initial reservation, resizing
and automatic growth when they reach these failures; failure does not always
mean the enclosing operation terminates.
sockbuf-listen-limit: listening socket, SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF option, requested
bytes, current sb_max_adj. Listening sockets take a separate setsockopt path.
The sysctl-controlled limit is context and can change concurrently; it is not
an atomic snapshot of the earlier comparison. No actual mbufs are allocated
by these reservation operations.

socket-alloc-failure: cause.
  1 = socket-zone M_NOWAIT allocation returned NULL (capacity or memory).
  2 = MAC socket initialization returned an error; requires MAC.
  3 = socket helper object-specific data initialization failed.
  4 = socket creation helper hook rejected creation; requires SOCKET_HHOOK.
These are distinct allocation/initialization failures, not all OOM. The shared
soalloc path covers explicit creation and inbound child allocation. No socket
pointer is exposed before full initialization, and no new dereference occurs
after cleanup. This probe identifies the stage, not the underlying errno.
socket-accept-wait-start/done: listening socket (done adds raw msleep return),
around each actual accept-queue sleep. Nonblocking/ready/error early paths
are excluded. A wake does not promise that a connection is ready or accepted;
the caller may recheck and sleep again.

bufcache-throttle-start/done: kind.
  1 = waitrunningbufspace: async-write backlog exceeds hirunningspace.
  2 = bwillwrite: severe dirty-buffer pressure before vnode locking.
Pairs enclose each actual sleep, not the complete write. Done means the wait
returned, not that pressure is gone, a write completed or a barrier reached
stable storage. Kind 2 is driven by the existing per-domain dirty bitmap, not
a newly sampled global buffer count. Existing non-interruptible sleep results
remain ignored, as before. No racy counters are added to the probe arguments.

AIO pairs report each actual sleep rather than complete operation latency:
aio-rundown-wait-start: process identity, sampled active AIO count.
aio-rundown-wait-done: process identity, raw msleep return. Rundown waits one
second at a time while cancellation/draining proceeds and may repeat. The
count is contextual and does not include every queued-but-inactive request;
a zero count does not imply the process has no queued jobs.
aio-list-wait-start: process identity, unfinished list-job count excluding the
list's sentinel reference, sampled under the existing AIO lock.
aio-list-wait-done: process identity, raw msleep return, before any ERESTART
to EINTR conversion. Only LIO_WAIT's actual sleeps are covered.
aio-completion-wait-start: process identity, timeout ticks (zero = indefinite).
aio-completion-wait-done: process identity, raw msleep return before the
existing timeout/restart conversion. The nonblocking branch is excluded.
These done results describe waking, not the I/O status later returned/reaped.

Profiles
--------
socket-resource-failures: reservation causes, requested sizes, contextual
limits, listener buffer failures and allocation/initialization stages.
socket-accept-waits: accept sleep latency and raw wake results.
buffer-write-throttle: sleep latency and wake count by throttle kind.
aio-drain-waits: rundown/list/completion sleep latencies, wake errors and
contextual outstanding counts or configured timeout ticks.

Profiles use scalar arguments only, without pointers, addresses, credentials,
paths or payloads in output. PID/TID/depth keys handle nested synchronous pairs
and clear matched state. Start before the workload; dropped events invalidate
pairing. Elapsed time includes scheduling and reacquiring existing locks.
PID filters observe the executing thread, which may be a network worker for
allocation failures. The throttle pair uses one thread-local logical stack
because the calls are synchronous; this also fits DTrace's register budget.

Validation
----------
Artifacts, exact originals and session.patch:
  /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-eighteenth-validation
Maintained validation scripts in this directory:
  coverage-eighteenth-compile.py: four native sources, tracing hooks on/off;
    eight successful Werror compilations using prepared kernel flags.
  coverage-eighteenth-codegen.py: disabled-tracing .text bytes match all four
    originals after diagnostic file/line normalization. This does not prove
    relocation equivalence, cross-architecture behavior or live overhead.
  coverage-eighteenth-check.py: source hashes, profile selector/argument arity,
    eight offline DTrace compiles (normal and PID/duration-filtered), executable
    SDT NOP sites in enabled objects and absent contracts with hooks disabled.
  coverage-eighteenth-variants.py: no-MAC/no-SOCKET_HHOOK and SOCKET_HHOOK-on,
    each with hooks on/off (four builds); verify 2/4 allocation failure sites
    when enabled and zero with hooks disabled.
  coverage-eighteenth-linked.py: validate all 13 contracts and their 18 native
    executable NOP sites in the final amd64 kernel ELF, including target PCs.

The prepared GENERIC-NODEBUG kernel links under the standard /usr/obj root,
using all available logical CPUs (-j16), with SRCTOP=/usr/src. No additional
modules are changed this round. The session patch preserves previous changes
and passes git apply --check --reverse against the final working tree.

/dev/dtrace is unavailable. Offline validation does not qualify live firing,
enabled-trace overhead or induced failures. No fault injection, forced OOM,
limit changes, module loading, installation, publication or reboot occurred.
